   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 498 of the laws of 2011 amending the education law\nrelating to the public library construction grant program, in relation to the\neffectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5603",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5603-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 59,
       "fullName": "Patrick M. Gallivan",
       "imgName": "427_patrick_m._gallivan.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 427,
       "sessionMemberId": 1417,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "GALLIVAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, Chap 498 of 2011",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5603\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 498 of the laws of 2011 amend-\ning the education law relating to the public library construction grant\nprogram, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend certain provisions of the public\nlibrary construction grant program.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill extends for 3 years the provisions of the public library\nconstruction grant program that were enacted in 2011, which raised the\npossible portion of a project the State would pay to 75% of the total\nproject cost.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTo better address the needs of libraries, Chapter 498 of the Laws of\n2011, which took effect in April 2011, made two changes to the way that\npublic library construction grants are used and awarded:\n*First, it authorized funding of up to 75% of project costs from the\nstate for construction projects by public libraries located in econom-\nically disadvantaged communities.Some communities had difficulty meeting\nthe previous 5o% match requirement for existing critical infrastructure\nneeds, let alone new construction.\n*Second, the 2011 law allowed state matching funds to be used for the\nacquisition of vacant lands, which can be a very important mechanism for\npotential neighborhood revitalization projects in abandoned city\ncenters.\nThese provisions are scheduled to sunset on March 31, 2020. The bill\nextends the provisions another 3 years (to March 31, 2023).\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act takes effect immediately.\n",
